WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.475
OpenAI: Sam Altman’s essays.

2
00:00:02.755 --> 00:00:06.979
OpenAI’s CEO publishes shorter, more aphoristic essays.

3
00:00:07.259 --> 00:00:17.840
“Reflections” (January 2025), covered in Studio 4, predicted that 2025 might see the first AI agents “join the workforce.

4
00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:26.845
” “The Gentle Singularity” (10 June 2025) argued that the takeoff had already started and would feel gradual from the inside.

5
00:00:27.125 --> 00:00:45.493
It gave a year-by-year sketch: 2025 brought agents that do real cognitive work, especially writing code; 2026 would likely bring systems that can figure out novel insights; 2027 might bring robots that do tasks in the real world.

6
00:00:45.773 --> 00:00:53.517
It expected the 2030s to be wildly different in important ways, with intelligence and energy becoming abundant.

7
00:00:53.797 --> 00:01:05.808
Altman’s predictions are harder to score than Amodei’s because they are softer (“likely,” “may”), and his 2026 public statements have mostly come through interviews rather than new essays.

8
00:01:06.088 --> 00:01:12.125
That softness is worth noticing as a reader: a claim that cannot fail is not much of a forecast.
